# SHELL SCRIPTS ARE ALWAYS LF, ON EVERY CHECKOUT. v0.3.8.124.
#
# WHAT HAPPENED. `scripts/release.sh` — the release path the tag guard's own error message points
# operators at — could not run on Windows at all. Git's `core.autocrlf` converts text files to CRLF
# on checkout, bash reads the trailing `\r` as part of each command, and the failure is unreadable:
#
#     : invalid option nameine 17: set: pipefail
#     scripts/release.sh: line 19: $'\r': command not found
#
# That is not a Windows problem or a developer's misconfiguration. A `.sh` file is executed by bash
# wherever it lives — Git Bash, WSL, CI, an LXC container — and CRLF is never correct for one. The
# repository is the right place to say so, because `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine setting and a
# rule that depends on every clone being configured the same way is not a rule.
#
# DELIBERATELY NARROW. There is no `* text=auto` here. Adding one would renormalize every tracked
# file in the repository on the next checkout — a diff touching thousands of lines, mixed into
# whatever release happened to carry it, for a problem that only ever affected five files. The five
# are named by extension so a sixth added tomorrow is covered without anybody remembering.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AMENDED 2026-09-10, ahead of the monorepo consolidation. The paragraph above is right about the
# risk and wrong about the number, and it is worth leaving both in view rather than pretending the
# original call was made badly.
#
# Measured before touching anything: 599 of 940 tracked files here read `i/lf w/crlf` — LF in the
# index, CRLF on disk. That looks exactly like the thousand-line renormalize the paragraph above
# refuses to sign off on. It isn't. The index is already LF on every one of them, because
# `core.autocrlf=input` has been sitting in .git/config normalizing on the way in this entire time.
# Turning on `* text=auto eol=lf` moves two files. Two. Not thousands.
#
# So the narrowness was buying nothing, and it was quietly costing something. anthill was only
# clean because of a value in .git/config — which is the exact "rule that depends on every clone
# being configured the same way" the paragraph above calls not a rule. It was just aimed at the
# wrong setting. .git/config does not survive a subtree merge into the monorepo. This file does.
# So the rule lives here now, where it travels with the code.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Text is LF in the repo and LF on disk, everywhere, no exceptions worth arguing about.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh text eol=lf

# The same reasoning inverted, and worth stating rather than leaving to `text=auto`'s guess: a
# `.cmd` or `.bat` is read by the Windows command interpreter, which requires CRLF on the lines it
# parses. These are the one case where the conversion is the correct behaviour.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shebang files that carry no extension. `.githooks/pre-push` is the one that bit: the *.sh rule
# above never matched it, so the hook was one bad checkout away from the same `$'\r': command not
# found` garbage that started all this. Named explicitly so it cannot drift back.
.githooks/* text eol=lf
build.sh text eol=lf

# Deployed onto Linux boxes and read by systemd and polkit, both of which are humourless about \r.
*.template text eol=lf
deploy/lxc/** text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Never transform these. Signing certs and packed artifacts get mangled silently, which is the
# worst way for a thing to break.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tar binary
*.tgz binary
*.7z binary
*.nupkg binary
*.snupkg binary
*.pfx binary
*.snk binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary
*.bin binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
